 like i need to look in  i tell them that i'm about to go into  your workspace you may want to save your  files  not that not them not saving the files  will be an issue but students get  worried if they get kicked out  with unsaved files so i remind them to  do so  and then peek into their workspace and  then let them know again when i  get out of there on the online  discussion forum or wherever we're  conversing  um that this is what i'm doing another  way i've used this  is actually um on a synchronous call  something like a zoom call for example i  tell the student okay this is i'm going  to go into that it's going to kick you  out  but they can actually watch me work in  their  project and see what changes i've made  to debug the issue that they were having  what's the process for sharing data  files just load them into the workspace  um so the process for sharing data files  is you load them into the project you  create a new project  that is going to be an exercise or an  assignment your students are working on  and using the files pane in the  rstudio ide you can upload any files  directly into there  a file size constraint so i think the  issue  more so than file size constraints is  the memory  um so you may want to think about not  just your  data file size but what you're going to  be do with  to be doing with it if you are simply  running you know depart commands  a lot large data file is not going to be  an issue  but if you're going to be running  bootstrap like bootstrap sampling on  that  uh on a large data frame with many rows  and doing so over tens of thousands of  times then that might actually be an  issue  and that's why i mentioned you may want  to test out what it is that you want to  do in terms of the assignment  how do you load a data set or existing  files into the cloud so i think this is  that same  question so on the files pane  um there is a button that says upload  and that's how you do that  um can we run code chunks in  python or c um  the answer should be yes  it's running the r studio ide so yes you  can  run python chunks i'm fairly certain  this is true  now i have to test that out  um you said that sharing a project with  students  there are two main ways in the first way  they have to make a copy of the project  in order to not to lose the changes  can you show how to do this so let's go  back  uh real quick so this is the student  view  of the project so  let's go to the very beginning of this  your student comes to the project that  you shared with them  um they log on and i'm going to pause  the screen when i need to in a second  so over here they can see that  the project is temporary and this is  blinking  and next to it they can see a button  that says save a permanent copy  when they click on save a permanent copy  that blinking temporary project is going  to go away  and what that does at that instance is  it makes a copy of that project that  places them into their  own like personal workspace that i call  the sandbox at the beginning of the  presentation  another question i had a problem with  our studio connect  our studio cloud to connect it to  relational databases  um okay i i  let's leave that question out to the um  our studio community discussion thread  that we'll have  available for this because i don't know  off the top of my head  as i haven't tried this myself before in  my teaching  so i'm going to hold off on answering  the question about using our studio  cloud and relational databases  another question is i assume the  students can download files locally as  typically they will need to submit  assignments in the lms that's absolutely  correct they can download the files  using the files pane check the boxes  next to the files that they want to  download  click on more and then export which will  export them out as a zip file  which i find is an attractive feature  because then they simply need to upload  that zip file onto your learning  management system  some of the learning management systems  i've worked with in the past  sometimes don't like certain extensions  like html  or our markdown they may not know what  it is but usually are okay with zip  folders so  it doesn't matter how you have them what  you have them knit their files to  usually a zipped file upload is  something that an lms  accepts  do i need a separate space for each  group of students if i have more than  one  so i think this depends on what you mean  by  group if the students are all exposed to  the same material in the class  and the same assignments at that point  you should not need  separate spaces even if they're saying  different workshop groups or something  however if you have more than one  classes that you're teaching  so these are going to be separate  content for each of the classes separate  assignments  you probably don't want to confuse the  students  with assignments for another class in  which case you would need to have  separate workspaces is it possible to  correct homeworks  automatically or do we need to visualize  students answers  there is no built-in auto grading  that is built into our studio cloud if  you are  interested in doing auto grading with  work with r  i think the most promising thing and you  need this student  code to be run i suppose uh the most  promising thing i can think of is if you  teach with version control  you can have your students push their  work to a github repository  and you could run continuous integration  tools like github actions on it  to provide um instant feedback to the  students or to do auto grading  this is not necessarily a fully solved  problem just yet  but that seems to be the most um kind of  doable option for going forward on that  uh but in terms of  grading student work on our studio cloud  your option is to go into their project  and start working through it  or you have them submit the output file  through some other means and then you  simply evaluate the output file it  depends on  what it is that you are evaluating them  on  it seems like we do not need to request  anything if you're if our group is under  25 students  um if your group  is under 10 students you do not need to  request anything otherwise by default  in a workspace you can only have up to  10 students or i guess 9 students  because  one of the seats is taken by you  um another question is from uh somebody  who's teaching intro to deep learning  course  and it looks like it could solve a lot  of issues if  we have um six years  to use our studio cloud but they will  probably need substantial computation  time is there anything i should take  into account specifically on the  computation side of things in our studio  cloud  so i think this is where we come to the  memory conversation here so you are  probably going to need to request  higher memory limits and that's  something that what i would recommend  that you do  is create a workspace put some of your  materials  in a project within that workspace try  running through it  and see what's choking and if it is the  case that the memory allocated by  default the one gig is not sufficient  then i would reach out to support  rstudio.cloud  to describe your use case um i know  that we have so that would require an  increased memory limit so that's  something that you're going to need to  request ahead of time  thank you so much
 

